Module Std.Files

== Text files: the File type and the basic operations (canon r3.64 module
== pairs: Std.X holds a subject's type, data and fundamental operations;
== Std.XUtils the conveniences). They are compiler intrinsics, visible in a
== program that uses Std.Files or Std.IOUtils, so their names stay free
== elsewhere.
==
==   File                            == an open text file, owned by the routine that holds it
==   OpenRead(Path String) File      == an existing file, for reading
==   Create(Path String) File        == a new or truncated file, for writing
==   OpenAppend(Path String) File    == a new or existing file, writing at its end
==   ReadLn(F File) String           == the next line without its line end; "" at the end
==   Eof(F File) Bool                == true when nothing is left to read
==   Write(F File, Values...)        == the values, as Put prints them
==   WriteLn(F File, Values...)      == the values and one line end, as PutLn
==   Close(F File)                   == flush and close now (also automatic)
==
== Text is UTF-8; lines end with "\n" when written, and "\n" or "\r\n" are
== accepted when read.
== Rules of a File:
==   - it is closed when the routine that holds it exits by any path: the
==     end, Return, Exit or an exception; Close(F) closes it earlier, and
==     closing twice does nothing;
==   - it is never copied: it is a local variable that receives a file just
==     opened (F := OpenRead(Path)), or a ref mut parameter. It cannot be a
==     struct field, an array element, a State variable or a result, and it
==     cannot be compared.
== Every failure raises FileNotFound, PermissionDenied, AlreadyExists,
== DiskFull or IOError (Std.Errors), handled with try, except On, Else,
== ensure and Retry like any exception.
